dimension_id,dimension_name,purpose,designed_metrics,computed_measurement,source_evidence,physical_test,trained_sensory_requirement,magnitude_rules,can_upgrade_class,can_block_class,common_false_positive,measurement_boundary
D1,Recognizer complexity,"Measures how many independent, distributed paths establish and preserve the named identity or core motif.",distinct recognizer systems; distributed recognizer paths; source-supported paths; single-point dependencies,"Function-graph path count, identity dependency graph, computational ablation of recognizer paths.","Official architecture, analytical/documentary identities, supplier or literature role evidence; notes alone are constraints, not molecule proof.",Coded full-versus-omission and recognizer-preservation comparisons across relevant timepoints.,"Required to claim actual recognizability, identity continuity, or reference similarity.",LOW: one unsupported or single-point path. MODERATE: one distributed path or two partial paths. HIGH: at least two independent supported paths with no single-point dependency. VERY_HIGH: at least three paths supporting multiple motifs.,YES,YES,Many rows assigned to the same note without independent functions.,Computational work can establish designed paths only; recognizability remains observational.
D2,Structural complexity,"Measures independent sensory systems, functions, bridges, anti-defect controls, and late identity mechanisms.",distinct sensory systems; independent functional units; cross-system bridges; anti-defect mechanisms; late-identity mechanisms,Deduplicated function graph and system coverage after row-accounting rules.,"Role evidence for each row and system, plus target architecture evidence where relevant.",Block omission and recombination to determine whether systems integrate and remain functionally distinct.,Needed to claim perceived integration or structural coherence.,"LOW: one or two systems. MODERATE: at least three systems with one bridge. HIGH: at least five systems, multiple bridges, an anti-defect mechanism, and late support. VERY_HIGH: six or more systems with multiple independent motifs and control routes.",YES,YES,A large generic wood-musk chassis counted as many systems.,Graph breadth is computable; perceived coherence is not.
D3,Interaction complexity,"Measures documented cross-system effects, effect-type diversity, configuration risk, suppression, and collision management.",documented edges; source-supported edges; proposed edges; physically observed edges; effect-type diversity; collision holds,Interaction graph coverage and evidence-state counts; no universal additive interaction score.,"Primary/domain literature, patents as precedent, supplier evidence, or clearly labeled structured hypotheses.","A, B, and controlled A:B ratios at fixed total loading, repeated across concentration and time.","Required to claim enhancement, suppression, masking, configuration, or null results in the actual formula.",LOW: isolated modules or generic hypothesis edges only. MODERATE: at least two cross-system edges. HIGH: at least six edges across three effect types. VERY_HIGH: at least ten edges across four effect types with observed collision control.,YES,YES,Counting all 253 Atlas pairs as evidence for one formula.,Literature or Atlas records establish hypotheses; formula-specific interaction is physical evidence.
D4,Temporal complexity,"Measures designed time windows, supported transitions, phase handoffs, and late/residual identity.",designed windows; supported transitions; modeled-only transitions; observed timepoints; repeated-day observations,Phase-role graph and release-model hypotheses with explicit matrix and uncertainty.,"Volatility/release evidence, target time-course descriptions, supplier or analytical data.","Standardized coded time-course evaluation, including repeated days where persistence claims matter.","Required to claim opening, heart, drydown, residue quality, or measured persistence.","LOW: one window. MODERATE: two windows and one bridge. HIGH: at least three windows and two transitions. VERY_HIGH: at least four windows, three transitions, and physically supported late identity.",YES,YES,Assigning phase labels to rows without demonstrating a transition.,Designed phase coverage is computable; actual evolution and 8/24-hour identity require observation.
D5,Textural complexity,"Measures independent tactile or spatial qualities such as grain, wax, root, leaf, cream, suede, mineral air, powder, smoke, or skin.",declared texture axes; source-supported axes; physically observed axes; unvalidated microtexture rows,Deduplicated texture-role mapping and ablation hypotheses.,"Material descriptions, target evidence, formula ancestry, literature, or supplier technical evidence.",Targeted omission/perturbation with texture lexicon and standardized presentation.,Required to claim actual textural richness or a material textural contribution in the full mixture.,"LOW: one generic texture. MODERATE: two distinct axes. HIGH: at least three axes with nonredundant support. VERY_HIGH: at least four axes across phases, with physical confirmation.",YES,YES when the requested class relies on unconfirmed microtexture,Trace rows with poetic texture labels but no testable effect.,Texture labels can define hypotheses; full-mixture texture is sensory evidence.
D6,Contrast complexity,"Measures independent oppositions that create articulation without collapse, such as cool/warm, dry/creamy, bright/dark, natural/abstract, or clean/animalic.",declared contrast axes; source-supported axes; physically observed axes; collapsed axes,Role-vector separation and phase placement; no single cosine or JSD threshold is universal.,Target architecture and material/function evidence.,Coded contrast-preservation tests and sibling anti-collapse comparisons.,"Required to claim perceived contrast, distinctiveness, or anti-collapse.",LOW: no stable contrast. MODERATE: one to two designed axes. HIGH: at least two preserved axes across phases. VERY_HIGH: at least three axes with physical anti-collapse support.,YES,YES,Top-note-only differences over cloned base architecture.,Vector separation is diagnostic; perceptual distinction requires sensory evidence.
D7,Resilience complexity,"Measures distributed controls that preserve identity and structure under dose, stock, matrix, application, and natural-lot uncertainty.",uncertainty sources; computational scenarios; critical failure modes; independent control paths; physical replicates,"Deterministic perturbation, uncertainty propagation, and mutation testing with provenance and convergence checks.","Stock specifications, natural variability evidence, measurement uncertainty, matrix evidence.","Replicate batches, dose variants, repeated-day assessments, and condition changes.",Required to claim sensory robustness or broad evaluator stability.,LOW: one fragile control or unbounded uncertainty. MODERATE: key hazards mapped. HIGH: no single-point dependency and multiple control paths. VERY_HIGH: computational and repeated physical resilience agree.,NO; resilience protects a class but does not make a simple formula complex,YES,A high Monte Carlo pass probability built from invented or narrow distributions.,Simulation validates the declared model only; empirical resilience requires physical replication.
D8,Effective post-ablation complexity,"Measures the complexity that survives deduplication, redundancy pruning, and exclusion of unconfirmed microtexture.",pre-ablation candidates; computational survivors; physical-omission-confirmed survivors; redundant removals; class retention,"One-row and block ablation against declared recognizer, structure, temporal, and contrast metrics.",Row rationale and dependency graph.,Omission tests for rows or blocks whose contribution cannot be established computationally.,"Required to confirm textural, recognizer, interaction, and hedonic losses in the full mixture.",LOW: class depends on padding or unconfirmed traces. MODERATE: most functional layers survive. HIGH: requested profile survives computational pruning. VERY_HIGH: profile survives both computational and physical omission.,YES,YES,Counting every pre-ablation row as meaningful.,Computational ablation cannot establish perceptibility; unconfirmed microtexture is excluded from effective complexity.
D9,Execution complexity,Measures practical preparation burden without confusing difficulty of compounding with meaningful odor complexity.,operations; premixes; microdoses; sub-resolution doses; unresolved stock identities; solubility/phase holds,"Recipe operation graph, measurable-dose checks, carrier accounting, exact-stock mapping.","Canonical inventory, supplier documents, density/solubility records, preparation rules.","Actual compounding, dissolution, maturation, precipitation, haze, filtration, and batch repeatability.",Not required for arithmetic or handling; required only for sensory consequences of execution changes.,LOW: direct build. MODERATE: several premixes or exact-stock controls. HIGH: many microdoses or fragile operations. VERY_HIGH: unresolved or physically difficult execution.,NO,YES for an executable-build claim,Calling a 15-row formula high complexity because it needs many dilutions.,Execution burden is separate from artistic/structural complexity and cannot upgrade class.
D10,Hedonic complexity boundary,"Acts as a firewall so beauty, pleasure, broad appeal, artistry, memorability, and perceived quality are not inferred from formula architecture.",boundary state; prohibited inferences; linked sensory evaluation IDs; panel scope,Detect invalid claim transformations only; no computed hedonic magnitude.,"Aesthetic brief may be recorded as intent, not evidence of outcome.",Coded sensory evaluation at repeated timepoints.,Required for descriptive hedonic claims; broad appeal requires a relevant multi-person sample rather than one assessor.,"Not a magnitude dimension. Valid states are NOT_ASSESSED, SEPARATE_DATA_LINKED, PHYSICAL_SENSORY_REQUIRED, or INVALID_INFERENCE_DETECTED.",NO,YES if hedonic claims are embedded in a scientific complexity pass,"Using row count, interaction count, or a 92/100 design score as proof of beauty.",No formula-only route may pass a hedonic claim.
